1
00:01:07,730 --> 00:01:11,070
Thebes, City of the Living,

2
00:01:11,150 --> 00:01:13,860
crown Jewel of Pharaoh 
SETI the First.

3
00:01:16,990 --> 00:01:21,040
Home of Imhotep, 
Pharaoh's high priest,

4
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:23,250
keeper of the dead.

5
00:01:25,330 --> 00:01:28,710
Birthplace of Anck-Su-Namun, 
Pharaoh's mistress.

6
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:31,420
No other man was allowed 
to touch her...

7
00:01:54,530 --> 00:01:58,530
But for their love, they were 
willing to risk life itself.

8
00:03:47,810 --> 00:03:49,730
To resurrect Anck-Su-Namun,

9
00:03:49,810 --> 00:03:53,940
Imhotep and his priests broke into 
her crypt and stole her body.

10
00:03:54,730 --> 00:03:56,440
They raced deep into the desert,

11
00:03:56,530 --> 00:03:59,320
taking Anck-Su-Namun's corpse to 
Hamunaptra, City of the Dead,

12
00:03:59,400 --> 00:04:00,660
HAMUNAPTRA - 1290 B.C.

13
00:04:00,740 --> 00:04:03,700
ancient burial site for 
the sons of pharaohs

14
00:04:03,870 --> 00:04:06,830
and resting place for 
the wealth of Egypt.

15
00:04:09,830 --> 00:04:14,800
For his love, Imhotep dared the gods' 
anger by going deep into the city,

16
00:04:15,210 --> 00:04:19,170
where he took the black Book of the 
Dead from it's holy resting place.

17
00:04:20,680 --> 00:04:24,350
Anck-Su-Namun's soul had been 
sent to the dark underworld,

18
00:04:24,680 --> 00:04:29,690
her vital organs removed and placed 
in five sacred Canopic jars.

19
00:04:59,170 --> 00:05:02,090
Anck-Su-Namun's soul had 
come back from the dead,

20
00:05:03,390 --> 00:05:06,680
but Pharaoh's bodyguards had 
followed lmhotep and stopped him

21
00:05:06,760 --> 00:05:09,220
before the ritual 
could be completed.

22
00:05:24,450 --> 00:05:27,620
He was to remain sealed 
inside his sarcophagus,

23
00:05:27,910 --> 00:05:30,250
be undead for all of eternity.

24
00:05:30,830 --> 00:05:33,790
The Magi would never allow 
him to be released,

25
00:05:33,870 --> 00:05:37,920
for he would arise a walking 
disease, a plague upon mankind,

26
00:05:38,090 --> 00:05:41,340
an unholy flesh-eater with 
the strength of ages,

27
00:05:41,550 --> 00:05:45,760
power over the sands, and 
the Glory of invincibility.

28
00:06:18,420 --> 00:06:22,510
For 3,000 years, men and 
armies fought over this land,

29
00:06:22,630 --> 00:06:25,090
never knowing what 
evil lay beneath it.

30
00:06:41,980 --> 00:06:45,070
And for 3,000 years, 
we, the Magi,

31
00:06:45,150 --> 00:06:48,910
the descendants of Pharaoh's 
sacred bodyguards, kept watch.

32
00:07:03,050 --> 00:07:04,970
You just got promoted.

33
00:07:12,140 --> 00:07:13,640
Steady!

34
00:07:13,850 --> 00:07:17,980
- You're with me on this one, right? 
- Your strength gives me strength.

35
00:07:19,650 --> 00:07:20,980
Steady!

36
00:07:24,730 --> 00:07:27,320
Wait! Wait for me!

37
00:07:28,360 --> 00:07:29,910
Steady!

38
00:07:34,100 --> 00:07:36,500
Fire!

39
00:09:01,870 --> 00:09:03,790
Run, Beni! Run!

40
00:09:03,920 --> 00:09:06,750
Get inside! Get inside!

41
00:09:08,510 --> 00:09:11,170
Hey! Don't you close that door!

42
00:09:11,260 --> 00:09:13,300
Don't you close that door!

43
00:10:13,110 --> 00:10:16,990
You will die. You will die.

44
00:11:16,720 --> 00:11:20,180
CAIRO, EGYPT - 3 YEARS LATER

45
00:11:31,650 --> 00:11:33,570
Sacred stones,

46
00:11:34,280 --> 00:11:36,740
sculpture and aesthetics,

47
00:11:37,950 --> 00:11:42,740
Socrates, Seth, volume 
one, volume two,

48
00:11:43,080 --> 00:11:45,120
and volume three.

49
00:11:45,500 --> 00:11:47,250
And...

50
00:11:47,960 --> 00:11:51,460
Tuthmosis. What are 
you doing here?

51
00:11:52,250 --> 00:11:54,000
T. T, t, t...

52
00:11:54,250 --> 00:11:55,630
T.

53
00:11:56,920 --> 00:11:59,590
I'm going to put you 
where you belong!

54
00:12:16,900 --> 00:12:18,030
Help.

55
00:12:46,430 --> 00:12:47,560
Oops.

56
00:12:47,640 --> 00:12:49,770
What... How...

57
00:12:56,020 --> 00:12:58,070
Oh, look at this!

58
00:12:58,740 --> 00:13:00,820
Sons of the pharaohs!

59
00:13:01,110 --> 00:13:04,950
Give me frogs! Flies! Locusts!

60
00:13:05,160 --> 00:13:06,990
Anything but you!

61
00:13:07,830 --> 00:13:10,580
Compared to you, the other 
plagues were a Joy!

62
00:13:10,660 --> 00:13:13,370
I am so very sorry. 
It was an accident.

63
00:13:13,460 --> 00:13:17,750
My girl, when Rameses destroyed 
Syria, that was an accident.

64
00:13:18,130 --> 00:13:20,550
You are a catastrophe!

65
00:13:20,880 --> 00:13:22,680
Look at my library!

66
00:13:22,840 --> 00:13:24,890
Why do I put up with you?

67
00:13:25,600 --> 00:13:28,430
Well, you put up with 
me because I can...

68
00:13:28,520 --> 00:13:31,310
I can read and write 
Ancient Egyptian,

69
00:13:31,680 --> 00:13:35,100
and I can... I can decipher 
hieroglyphics and hieratic,

70
00:13:35,190 --> 00:13:38,730
and, well, I am the only 
person within 1,000 Miles

71
00:13:38,820 --> 00:13:42,070
who knows how to properly code and 
catalog this library, that's why.

72
00:13:42,150 --> 00:13:45,780
I put up with you because your father 
and mother were our finest patrons.

73
00:13:45,870 --> 00:13:47,240
That's why!

74
00:13:47,530 --> 00:13:49,450
Allah rest their souls.

75
00:13:50,870 --> 00:13:54,540
Now, I don't care how you do it, 
I don't care how long it takes,

76
00:13:54,620 --> 00:13:57,090
straighten up this meshiver!

77
00:14:10,890 --> 00:14:12,100
Hello?

78
00:14:38,170 --> 00:14:39,420
Abdul?

79
00:14:41,630 --> 00:14:42,920
Mohammed?

80
00:14:46,010 --> 00:14:47,090
Bob?

81
00:15:07,660 --> 00:15:10,490
Have you no respect 
for the dead?

82
00:15:12,410 --> 00:15:16,100
Of course I do. But sometimes 
I'd rather like to join them.

83
00:15:17,080 --> 00:15:21,540
Well, I wish you would do it before you 
ruin my career the way you've ruined yours.

84
00:15:21,630 --> 00:15:24,960
- Now, get out. - My 
dear, sweet baby sister.

85
00:15:25,050 --> 00:15:27,220
I'll have you know

86
00:15:27,300 --> 00:15:30,350
that, at this precise moment, 
my career is on a high note.

87
00:15:30,430 --> 00:15:33,720
High note, ha! Jonathan, please, I'm 
really not in the mood for you.

88
00:15:33,810 --> 00:15:36,230
I've just made a bit of 
a mess in the library,

89
00:15:36,310 --> 00:15:39,230
and the Bembridge scholars have 
rejected my application form again.

90
00:15:39,310 --> 00:15:42,440
They say I don't have enough 
experience in the field.

91
00:15:47,320 --> 00:15:49,660
You'll always have me, old mum.

92
00:15:52,990 --> 00:15:55,660
Besides, I have just the 
thing to cheer you up.

93
00:15:55,750 --> 00:15:59,170
Oh, no, Jonathan, not 
another worthless trinket.

94
00:15:59,250 --> 00:16:02,250
If I have to take one more piece 
of junk to the curator to try and

95
00:16:02,340 --> 00:16:03,880
sell for you.

96
00:16:07,720 --> 00:16:09,680
Where did you get this?

97
00:16:10,260 --> 00:16:12,890
On a dig down in Thebes.

98
00:16:13,350 --> 00:16:15,140
My whole life, I've never 
found anything, Evy.

99
00:16:15,180 --> 00:16:17,770
Please tell me I've 
found something.

100
00:16:21,940 --> 00:16:24,320
- Jonathan. 
- Yes?

101
00:16:25,320 --> 00:16:27,400
I think you've found something.

102
00:16:31,530 --> 00:16:32,950
You see the cartouche there?

103
00:16:33,030 --> 00:16:36,540
It's the official Royal seal of 
SETI the First, I'm sure of it.

104
00:16:36,620 --> 00:16:38,620
- Perhaps. 
- Two questions.

105
00:16:38,710 --> 00:16:41,460
Who the hell was SETI the 
First, and was he rich?

106
00:16:41,540 --> 00:16:43,880
He was the second pharaoh 
of the 19th dynasty,

107
00:16:43,960 --> 00:16:45,750
said to be the wealthiest 
pharaoh of them all.

108
00:16:45,800 --> 00:16:47,300
Good. I like this fellow.

109
00:16:47,380 --> 00:16:49,800
- I like him very much. 
- I've already dated the map.

110
00:16:49,880 --> 00:16:51,430
It's almost 3,000 years old.

111
00:16:51,510 --> 00:16:55,890
And if you look at the hieratic 
just here, well, it's Hamunaptra.

112
00:16:56,220 --> 00:16:58,470
Dear God, don't be ridiculous.

113
00:16:58,890 --> 00:17:01,270
We're scholars, not 
treasure hunters.

114
00:17:01,390 --> 00:17:04,520
Hamunaptra's a myth told by 
ancient Arab storytellers

115
00:17:04,610 --> 00:17:06,190
to amuse Greek and 
Roman tourists.

116
00:17:06,270 --> 00:17:10,650
I know all the blather about the city 
being protected by the curse of a mummy,

117
00:17:10,740 --> 00:17:15,490
but my research has led me to believe that 
the city itself may have actually existed.

118
00:17:15,580 --> 00:17:19,620
- Are we talking about the Hamunaptra? 
- Yes. The City of the Dead.

119
00:17:19,790 --> 00:17:22,960
Where the earliest pharaohs were said 
to have hidden the wealth of Egypt?

120
00:17:23,040 --> 00:17:26,250
Yes, yes, in a big, 
underground treasure chamber.

121
00:17:26,420 --> 00:17:29,260
- Heh! - Oh, come on. 
- Everybody knows the story.

122
00:17:29,340 --> 00:17:32,090
The entire necropolis was rigged to sink 
into the sand on Pharaoh's command?

123
00:17:32,180 --> 00:17:35,180
A flick of a switch, and the whole place 
would disappear beneath the sand dunes,

124
00:17:35,260 --> 00:17:36,510
taking the treasure with it?

125
00:17:36,600 --> 00:17:39,520
As the Americans would say, 
it's all fairy tales and hokum.

126
00:17:39,600 --> 00:17:42,020
Oh, my goodness! Look at that!

127
00:17:49,230 --> 00:17:52,150
You'be burnt it! You've burned 
off the part with the lost city?

128
00:17:52,320 --> 00:17:54,490
It's for the best, I'm sure.

129
00:17:54,780 --> 00:17:58,830
Many men have wasted their lives in 
the foolish pursuit of Hamunaptra.

130
00:17:58,910 --> 00:18:00,100
No one's ever found it.

131
00:18:01,120 --> 00:18:03,660
Most have never returned.

132
00:18:04,040 --> 00:18:06,630
Come, come! Step 
over the threshold.

133
00:18:06,710 --> 00:18:09,380
Welcome to Cairo Prison, 
my humble home.

134
00:18:09,460 --> 00:18:12,300
You told me that you got it 
on a dig down in Thebes.

135
00:18:12,380 --> 00:18:14,050
- Yeah, well, I was mistaken. 
- You lied to me.

136
00:18:14,130 --> 00:18:16,390
I lie to everybody. What 
makes you so special?

137
00:18:16,470 --> 00:18:18,550
- I am your sister. - That 
just makes you more gullible.

138
00:18:18,640 --> 00:18:20,520
You stole it from a drunk 
at the local casbah?

139
00:18:20,600 --> 00:18:23,140
Picked his pocket, actually, so I 
don't think it's a very good...

140
00:18:23,180 --> 00:18:26,310
Stop being so ridiculous. What 
exactly is this man in prison for?

141
00:18:26,400 --> 00:18:28,150
This I did not know.

142
00:18:28,230 --> 00:18:31,030
But when I heard that you were 
coming, I asked him that myself.

143
00:18:31,610 --> 00:18:32,740
And what did he say?

144
00:18:32,820 --> 00:18:36,280
He said he was just 
looking for a good time.

145
00:18:41,990 --> 00:18:44,580
- This is the man that you 
stole it from? - Yes, exactly?

146
00:18:44,660 --> 00:18:46,790
So why don't we just go sniff 
out a spot of tiff in...

147
00:18:46,830 --> 00:18:48,290
Who are you?

148
00:18:49,790 --> 00:18:51,460
- And who's the broad? 
- "Broad"?

149
00:18:51,550 --> 00:18:55,760
I'm just a local sort of missionary 
chap, spreading the good word,

150
00:18:55,840 --> 00:18:58,720
- but this is my sister, Evy. 
- How do you do?

151
00:18:59,350 --> 00:19:03,060
- Oh, well. Guess she's not a 
total loss. - I beg your pardon?

152
00:19:03,810 --> 00:19:04,930
I'll be back in a moment?

153
00:19:05,020 --> 00:19:08,350
- Ask him about the box. 
- We have found...

154
00:19:08,690 --> 00:19:10,270
Hello. Excuse me?

155
00:19:10,360 --> 00:19:12,980
We both found your puzzle box,

156
00:19:13,440 --> 00:19:15,490
and we've come to 
ask you about it?

157
00:19:16,450 --> 00:19:18,610
- No. 
- No?

158
00:19:18,700 --> 00:19:22,580
No. You came to ask 
me about Hamunaptra.

159
00:19:23,660 --> 00:19:25,100
How do you know the box 
pertains to Hamunaptra?

160
00:19:26,080 --> 00:19:29,540
Because that's where I was 
when I found it. I was there.

161
00:19:30,250 --> 00:19:33,210
But how do we know that's 
not a load of pig's wallow?

162
00:19:33,710 --> 00:19:35,300
- Do I know you? 
- No, no.

163
00:19:35,380 --> 00:19:37,760
I've just got one 
of those faces.

164
00:19:43,010 --> 00:19:45,730
You were actually at Hamunaptra?

165
00:19:47,390 --> 00:19:49,150
- Yeah, I was there. 
- You swear?

166
00:19:49,230 --> 00:19:50,980
- Every damn day. - I 
didn't mean that...

167
00:19:51,060 --> 00:19:55,440
I know. I was there. SETI's 
place. City of the Dead.

168
00:19:56,240 --> 00:19:58,780
Could you tell me 
how to get there?

169
00:20:01,910 --> 00:20:04,370
I mean, the exact location?

170
00:20:04,660 --> 00:20:07,160
- You want to know? 
- Well, yes?

171
00:20:07,250 --> 00:20:09,710
- Do you really want to know? 
- Yes?

172
00:20:13,630 --> 00:20:16,050
Then get me the 
hell out of here!

173
00:20:19,300 --> 00:20:20,760
Do it, lady!

174
00:20:24,890 --> 00:20:27,730
- Where are they taking him? 
- To be hanged?

175
00:20:27,980 --> 00:20:30,850
Apparently, he had 
a very good time?

176
00:20:36,360 --> 00:20:38,860
I will give you £100 to 
save this man's life.

177
00:20:38,950 --> 00:20:42,370
Madame, I would pay £100 
just to see him hang.

178
00:20:42,450 --> 00:20:44,370
- £200. 
- Proceed!

179
00:20:44,490 --> 00:20:45,620
£300.

180
00:20:47,370 --> 00:20:49,540
Any last requests, pig?

181
00:20:49,790 --> 00:20:52,330
Yeah? Loosen the 
knot and let me go.

182
00:20:56,300 --> 00:20:57,710
Of course we don't let him go!

183
00:21:00,470 --> 00:21:01,760
£500!

184
00:21:03,970 --> 00:21:06,890
And what else? I'm 
a very lonely man.

185
00:21:12,100 --> 00:21:13,190
No!

186
00:21:20,530 --> 00:21:22,490
His neck did not break.

187
00:21:24,280 --> 00:21:28,240
Oh, I'm so sorry. Now, we must 
watch him strangle to death.

188
00:21:37,290 --> 00:21:39,710
He knows the location 
to Hamunaptra.

189
00:21:40,550 --> 00:21:42,720
- You lie. 
- I would never!

190
00:21:42,840 --> 00:21:46,010
Are you telling me this 
filthy, godless son of a pig

191
00:21:46,100 --> 00:21:48,220
- knows where to find the 
City of the Dead? - Yes!

192
00:21:48,310 --> 00:21:49,430
- Truly? 
- Yes!

193
00:21:49,520 --> 00:21:51,850
And if you cut him down, 
we will give you...

194
00:21:55,230 --> 00:21:56,900
- 10 % 
- 50 %

195
00:21:56,980 --> 00:21:58,610
- Twenty. 
- Forty.

196
00:21:58,690 --> 00:22:00,690
- Thirty! 
- Twenty five.

197
00:22:01,070 --> 00:22:02,190
Deal.

198
00:22:04,320 --> 00:22:05,820
Cut him down!

199
00:22:16,960 --> 00:22:19,800
GIZA PORT - CAIRO

200
00:22:21,210 --> 00:22:23,010
Do you really think 
he's going to show up?

201
00:22:23,090 --> 00:22:25,550
Yes, undoubtedly, 
knowing my luck.

202
00:22:26,220 --> 00:22:28,970
He may be a cowboy, but I know 
the breed. His word is his word.

203
00:22:29,060 --> 00:22:33,730
Well, personally I think he's 
filthy, rude, a complete scoundrel.

204
00:22:33,810 --> 00:22:36,310
- I don't like him one bit. 
- Anyone I know?

205
00:22:38,560 --> 00:22:39,650
Oh.

206
00:22:42,230 --> 00:22:43,360
Hello.

207
00:22:43,440 --> 00:22:46,160
Smashing day for the start of 
an adventure, eh, O'Connell?

208
00:22:46,240 --> 00:22:48,240
Yeah. Yeah, smashing.

209
00:22:48,700 --> 00:22:52,160
Oh, no, no, I'd never steal 
from a partner, partner.

210
00:22:53,370 --> 00:22:55,870
That reminds me? No hard 
feelings about the...

211
00:22:55,960 --> 00:22:59,170
- Oh, no, no. Happens all 
the time. - Mr. O'Connell?

212
00:22:59,250 --> 00:23:01,000
Can you look me in the 
eye and guarantee me

213
00:23:01,090 --> 00:23:03,920
this isn't some 
sort of a flimflam?

214
00:23:04,010 --> 00:23:07,390
- Because if it is, I am warning 
you... - You're warning me?

215
00:23:07,640 --> 00:23:09,220
Lady, let me put it this way,

216
00:23:09,300 --> 00:23:11,760
my whole damn Garrison 
believed in this so much

217
00:23:11,850 --> 00:23:14,640
that without orders, they marched 
halfway across Libya and into Egypt

218
00:23:14,730 --> 00:23:15,730
to find that city.

219
00:23:15,810 --> 00:23:19,650
When we got there, all we 
found was sand and blood.

220
00:23:20,860 --> 00:23:22,690
Let me get your bags.

221
00:23:27,610 --> 00:23:29,660
Yes, yes, you're right.

222
00:23:29,780 --> 00:23:33,370
Filthy, rude, a complete scoundrel. 
Nothing to like there at all.

223
00:23:35,370 --> 00:23:37,370
Bright good morning to all.

224
00:23:37,460 --> 00:23:39,710
Oh, no. What are you doing here?

225
00:23:39,790 --> 00:23:43,420
I'm here to protect my 
investment, thank you very much.

226
00:24:05,030 --> 00:24:08,070
Quit playing with your glasses 
and cut the deck, Burns.

227
00:24:08,150 --> 00:24:11,070
Without my glasses I can't see 
the deck to cut it, Dave.

228
00:24:11,160 --> 00:24:13,700
O'Connell, sit down. We 
could use another player.

229
00:24:13,780 --> 00:24:16,830
- I only gamble with my life, 
never my money. - Never?

230
00:24:17,040 --> 00:24:20,670
What if I was to bet you $500 we 
get to Hamunaptra before you?

231
00:24:20,750 --> 00:24:23,840
- You're looking for Hamunaptra? 
- Damn straight we are.

232
00:24:23,920 --> 00:24:26,170
- And who saya we are? 
- He does.

233
00:24:26,670 --> 00:24:29,010
Well... Well, how about it?

234
00:24:29,590 --> 00:24:31,010
Is it a bet?

235
00:24:32,970 --> 00:24:36,560
- All right, you're on. - What 
makes you so confident, Sir?

236
00:24:36,680 --> 00:24:39,890
- What makes you? - We got us a 
man who's actually been there.

237
00:24:40,020 --> 00:24:42,650
Oh, what a coincidence, 
because O'Connell...

238
00:24:43,060 --> 00:24:45,820
Whose play is it? Is it my play? 
I thought...

239
00:24:45,900 --> 00:24:49,320
Gentlemen, we got us a wager. 
Good evening, Jonathan.

240
00:24:49,700 --> 00:24:51,110
Night.

241
00:25:06,420 --> 00:25:08,340
Sorry. Didn't mean to scare you.

242
00:25:08,420 --> 00:25:11,470
The only thing that scares me, 
Mr. O'Connell, are your manners.

243
00:25:12,300 --> 00:25:16,560
- Still angry about that kiss, huh? 
- Well, if you call that a kiss.

244
00:25:22,060 --> 00:25:26,770
Did I miss something? Are we... 
Are we going into battle?

245
00:25:26,860 --> 00:25:29,280
Lady, there's 
something out there.

246
00:25:29,570 --> 00:25:31,320
Something underneath that sand.

247
00:25:31,400 --> 00:25:34,660
Yes, well, I'm hoping to 
find a certain artifact.

248
00:25:34,910 --> 00:25:37,910
A book, actually. My brother 
thinks there's treasure.

249
00:25:38,040 --> 00:25:42,580
- What do you think's out 
there? - In a word? Evil.

250
00:25:42,920 --> 00:25:46,420
The Bedouin and the Tuaregs believe 
that Hamunaptra is cursed.

251
00:25:46,500 --> 00:25:49,760
Oh, look, I don't believe in fairy 
tales and hokum, Mr. O'Connell,

252
00:25:49,840 --> 00:25:53,760
but I do believe one of the most famous 
books in history is buried there.

253
00:25:53,840 --> 00:25:55,300
The Book of Amun-Ra.

254
00:25:55,430 --> 00:25:59,140
It contains within it all the secret 
incantations of the old kingdom.

255
00:25:59,470 --> 00:26:02,060
It's what first interested me 
in Egypt when I was a child.

256
00:26:02,140 --> 00:26:05,600
It's why I came here. 
Sort of a life's pursuit.

257
00:26:05,770 --> 00:26:09,940
And the fact that they say it's made out 
of pure gold makes no never mind to you?

258
00:26:10,480 --> 00:26:12,240
- Right. - You 
know your history.

259
00:26:12,320 --> 00:26:14,110
I know my treasure.

260
00:26:18,580 --> 00:26:20,080
By the way,

261
00:26:20,100 --> 00:26:22,540
why did you kiss me?

262
00:26:22,620 --> 00:26:25,960
I was about to be hanged. It seemed 
like a good idea at the time.

263
00:26:29,630 --> 00:26:32,720
What? What'd I say?

264
00:26:40,010 --> 00:26:42,930
Surprise! My good 
friend, you're alive!

265
00:26:43,020 --> 00:26:47,310
- I was so very, very worried. - Well, 
if it ain't my little buddy, Beni.

266
00:26:48,020 --> 00:26:50,320
- I think I'll kill you. 
- Think of my children.

267
00:26:50,400 --> 00:26:52,320
You don't have any children.

268
00:26:52,400 --> 00:26:54,400
- Someday I might. 
- Shut up!

269
00:26:54,490 --> 00:26:56,820
So, you're the one who's 
leading the Americans?

270
00:26:56,910 --> 00:26:58,870
I might have known. So, 
what's the scam, Beni?

271
00:26:58,950 --> 00:27:02,370
You task them into the middle of the 
desert, and then you leave them to rot?

272
00:27:02,450 --> 00:27:04,160
Unfortunately, no.

273
00:27:04,410 --> 00:27:06,420
These Americans are smart.

274
00:27:06,500 --> 00:27:09,500
They pay me only half now, half 
when I get them back to Cairo.

275
00:27:09,590 --> 00:27:12,840
- So this time I must go all the 
way. - Them's the breaks, huh?

276
00:27:13,920 --> 00:27:16,840
You never believed in 
Hamunaptra, O'Connell.

277
00:27:17,380 --> 00:27:19,300
Why are you going back?

278
00:27:21,220 --> 00:27:24,020
You see that girl? 
She saved my neck.

279
00:27:28,900 --> 00:27:32,270
You always did have 
more balls than brains.

280
00:27:37,200 --> 00:27:39,280
Yeah. Goodbye, Beni.

281
00:27:46,290 --> 00:27:47,670
O'Connell!

282
00:28:00,090 --> 00:28:01,890
George Bembridge...

283
00:28:02,600 --> 00:28:05,020
In 1860...

284
00:28:08,480 --> 00:28:10,690
1865, was...

285
00:28:13,610 --> 00:28:14,780
Was...

286
00:28:14,860 --> 00:28:18,780
Oh, for heaven's sake, girl, it 
wasn't that good of a kiss, anyway.

287
00:28:32,790 --> 00:28:34,750
Where is the map?

288
00:28:35,750 --> 00:28:38,260
It's... It's... There.

289
00:28:38,470 --> 00:28:41,260
And the key? Where is the key?

290
00:28:41,760 --> 00:28:44,350
The key? The key? What key?

291
00:28:44,430 --> 00:28:45,680
Evelyn!

292
00:29:01,570 --> 00:29:03,620
The map! The map! 
I forgot the map!

293
00:29:03,700 --> 00:29:06,990
Relax. I'm the map? 
It's all up here?

294
00:29:07,080 --> 00:29:08,950
Oh, that's comforting.

295
00:29:16,960 --> 00:29:18,630
- The key! 
- Evy.

296
00:29:39,780 --> 00:29:41,400
Hold on to this.

297
00:30:10,180 --> 00:30:11,350
Can you swim?

298
00:30:11,430 --> 00:30:14,100
Of course I can swim, if 
the occasion calls for it.

299
00:30:14,190 --> 00:30:16,440
Trust me. It calls for it.

300
00:30:40,210 --> 00:30:43,300
O'Connell! O'Connell! 
What are we going to do?

301
00:30:43,380 --> 00:30:45,970
- Wait here! I'll go get help! 
- Right!

302
00:31:08,370 --> 00:31:09,910
Americans.

303
00:31:25,090 --> 00:31:27,430
I say, bloody good show, chaps!

304
00:31:28,550 --> 00:31:30,180
And did I panic?

305
00:31:30,390 --> 00:31:32,100
I think not.

306
00:31:44,230 --> 00:31:46,740
Get them out of the water! 
Get them out of the water!

307
00:31:46,820 --> 00:31:49,780
Come on, doggies! Give 
them a smack, would you?

308
00:31:53,370 --> 00:31:55,540
This is a messed-up country.

309
00:31:58,540 --> 00:32:02,500
We've lost everything! All of 
our tools, all the equipment!

310
00:32:02,710 --> 00:32:03,880
All my clothes!

311
00:32:03,960 --> 00:32:05,630
O'Connell!

312
00:32:06,840 --> 00:32:07,970
Hey!

313
00:32:08,470 --> 00:32:10,140
O'Connell!

314
00:32:10,220 --> 00:32:14,180
It looks to me like I've 
got all the horses!

315
00:32:14,640 --> 00:32:16,020
Hey, Beni!

316
00:32:16,100 --> 00:32:20,150
Looks to me like you're on 
the wrong side of the river!

317
00:32:20,310 --> 00:32:21,940
Yeah?

318
00:32:38,100 --> 00:32:41,250
I only want four! Four!

319
00:32:41,330 --> 00:32:43,920
I only want four, not 
a whole bloody herd!

320
00:32:44,000 --> 00:32:47,340
O'Connell! Can you 
believe the cheek?

321
00:32:47,420 --> 00:32:49,260
Just pay the man.

322
00:32:49,340 --> 00:32:51,260
Oh, for heaven's sake.

323
00:32:52,090 --> 00:32:54,390
Can't believe the price 
of these fleabags?

324
00:32:54,470 --> 00:32:57,930
Yes, happy? Very good.

325
00:32:58,020 --> 00:33:00,100
You probably could've 
got them for free.

326
00:33:00,190 --> 00:33:02,980
- All we had to do was give 
him your sister. - Yes.

327
00:33:03,650 --> 00:33:05,770
Awfully tempting, wasn't it?

328
00:33:12,490 --> 00:33:13,740
Awfully.

329
00:33:39,140 --> 00:33:40,810
Never did like camels.

330
00:33:40,890 --> 00:33:44,600
Filthy buggers. They smell, 
they bite, they spit.

331
00:33:45,150 --> 00:33:48,110
- Disgusting. - I think 
they're adorable.

332
00:33:50,400 --> 00:33:51,400
Oh, baby.

333
00:35:40,510 --> 00:35:42,680
This one is strong.

334
00:35:51,520 --> 00:35:53,530
Good morning, my friend.

335
00:36:09,710 --> 00:36:10,790
What the hell we doing?

336
00:36:10,880 --> 00:36:13,750
Patience, my good barat'm. 
Patience.

337
00:36:13,840 --> 00:36:18,550
Remember our bet, O'Connell? First 
one to the city, 500 cash bucks.

338
00:36:18,680 --> 00:36:21,140
A hundred of them bucks is yours 
if you help us win that bet.

339
00:36:21,220 --> 00:36:22,850
Oh, my pleasure.

340
00:36:23,390 --> 00:36:26,560
Hey, O'Connell. Nice camel.

341
00:36:30,980 --> 00:36:32,610
Get ready for it.

342
00:36:33,480 --> 00:36:36,400
- For what? - We're about 
to be shown the way.

343
00:36:50,420 --> 00:36:53,960
- Will you look at that? 
- Can you believe it?

344
00:36:54,290 --> 00:36:55,670
Hamunaptra.

345
00:36:55,750 --> 00:36:57,260
Here we go again.

346
00:37:31,750 --> 00:37:33,290
So long, Beni!

347
00:37:36,420 --> 00:37:38,300
That serves you right.

348
00:38:04,240 --> 00:38:06,530
Go, Evy! Go!

349
00:38:47,700 --> 00:38:49,620
Do they know something we don't?

350
00:38:50,540 --> 00:38:53,790
They are led by a woman. 
What does a woman know?

351
00:38:54,210 --> 00:38:56,250
That's a statue of Anubis.

352
00:38:56,540 --> 00:38:58,170
It's legs go deep underground.

353
00:38:58,250 --> 00:39:02,210
According to Bembridge scholars, that's 
where we'll find a secret compartment

354
00:39:02,300 --> 00:39:04,470
containing the Golden 
Book of Amun-Ra.

355
00:39:07,090 --> 00:39:10,140
Jonathan, you're meant to 
catch the sun with that.

356
00:39:12,060 --> 00:39:15,730
- So, what are these old mirrors 
for? - Ancient mirrors.

357
00:39:16,060 --> 00:39:19,020
It's an ancient Egyptian trick. 
You'll see.

358
00:39:20,230 --> 00:39:22,190
Here, this for you.

359
00:39:24,400 --> 00:39:27,530
Go ahead. It's something I borrowed 
off our American brethren.

360
00:39:27,610 --> 00:39:31,160
I thought you might like it, you 
might need it for when you're...

361
00:39:31,240 --> 00:39:34,500
Yeah? What are you looking at?

362
00:39:44,920 --> 00:39:47,590
Hey, look for bugs. I hate bugs.

363
00:39:48,550 --> 00:39:50,850
Do you realize we're 
standing inside a room

364
00:39:50,930 --> 00:39:53,180
no one has entered 
in over 3,000 years?

365
00:39:54,220 --> 00:39:56,600
What is that God-awful stench?

366
00:40:06,900 --> 00:40:09,990
And then there was light?

367
00:40:11,830 --> 00:40:13,950
Hey, that is a neat trick.

368
00:40:14,290 --> 00:40:16,910
- Oh, my God. It's a 
sah-netjer. - What?

369
00:40:16,100 --> 00:40:19,420
- A preparation room. 
- Preparation for what?

370
00:40:19,500 --> 00:40:21,630
For entering the afterlife.

371
00:40:23,130 --> 00:40:25,880
Mummies, my good son. This is 
where they made the mummies.

372
00:40:49,360 --> 00:40:51,160
The legs of Anubis.

373
00:40:52,280 --> 00:40:56,160
The secret compartment should be 
hidden somewhere inside here.

374
00:41:21,690 --> 00:41:23,860
You scared the bejesus 
out of us, O'Connell.

375
00:41:23,940 --> 00:41:25,230
Likewise.

376
00:41:25,320 --> 00:41:28,490
- Hey, that's my tool Kit. 
- No, I don't think so.

377
00:41:28,570 --> 00:41:31,530
Okay? Perhaps I was mistaken.

378
00:41:32,990 --> 00:41:34,530
Have a nice day, gentlemen.

379
00:41:34,620 --> 00:41:36,580
We have a lot of work to 
be getting along with.

380
00:41:36,660 --> 00:41:39,100
- Push off! This is our dig 
site? - We got here first.

381
00:41:41,100 --> 00:41:44,330
This here's our statue, friend.

382
00:41:44,420 --> 00:41:47,170
I don't see your name 
written on it, pal.

383
00:41:47,460 --> 00:41:50,510
Yes, well, there's 
only four of you

384
00:41:50,840 --> 00:41:52,840
and 15 of me.

385
00:41:53,050 --> 00:41:55,390
Your odds are not so 
great, O'Connell.

386
00:41:56,180 --> 00:41:58,430
- I've had worse. 
- Yeah, me, too.

387
00:42:00,180 --> 00:42:03,650
Oh, look, for goodness' sake, 
let's be nice, children.

388
00:42:04,190 --> 00:42:07,770
If we're going to play together, 
we must learn to share.

389
00:42:09,980 --> 00:42:12,280
There are other places to dig.

390
00:42:16,530 --> 00:42:20,330
According to these hieroglyphics, 
we're underneath the statue.

391
00:42:20,410 --> 00:42:22,210
We should come up right 
between his legs.

392
00:42:22,290 --> 00:42:24,210
When those damn Yanks 
go to sleep...

393
00:42:24,290 --> 00:42:25,830
- No offense. 
- None taken.

394
00:42:25,920 --> 00:42:27,340
We'll dig our way up 
and steal that book

395
00:42:27,420 --> 00:42:28,460
right out from under them?

396
00:42:28,500 --> 00:42:30,880
Are you sure we can find 
this secret compartment?

397
00:42:30,960 --> 00:42:33,840
Oh, yes, if those beastly Americans 
haven't beaten us to it.

398
00:42:33,930 --> 00:42:35,590
- No offense. 
- None taken.

399
00:42:35,680 --> 00:42:37,800
Where'd our smelly 
little friend get to?

400
00:42:46,560 --> 00:42:47,900
Let me get this straight.

401
00:42:47,980 --> 00:42:50,400
They ripped out your guts 
and stuffed them in jars?

402
00:42:50,480 --> 00:42:54,070
They'd take out your heart as well. You 
know how they took out your brains?

403
00:42:54,150 --> 00:42:55,780
I don't think we 
need to know this.

404
00:42:55,860 --> 00:42:59,410
They'd take a sharp, red-hot 
poker, stick it up your nose,

405
00:42:59,490 --> 00:43:03,080
scramble things asbout a bit, and then 
rip it all out through your nostrils.

406
00:43:03,160 --> 00:43:05,500
- That's gotta hurt. - It's 
called mummification.

407
00:43:05,580 --> 00:43:07,670
- You'll be dead when they 
do this. - For the record,

408
00:43:07,750 --> 00:43:10,800
if I don't make it out of here, don't 
put me down for mummification.

409
00:43:10,880 --> 00:43:12,170
Likewise.

410
00:43:21,350 --> 00:43:23,180
Oh, my God. It's a...

411
00:43:24,020 --> 00:43:25,810
It's a sarcophagus.

412
00:43:28,520 --> 00:43:30,860
Buried at the base of Anubis.

413
00:43:32,860 --> 00:43:35,900
He must have been someone 
of great importance.

414
00:43:37,070 --> 00:43:40,030
Or he did something 
very naughty.

415
00:43:42,410 --> 00:43:44,040
Well, who is it?

416
00:43:46,210 --> 00:43:48,670
"He That Shall Not Be Named."

417
00:43:53,340 --> 00:43:55,260
This looks like some 
sort of a lock.

418
00:43:55,340 --> 00:43:57,430
Well, whoever's in here 
sure wasn't getting out.

419
00:43:57,510 --> 00:43:59,140
Yeah, no kidding.

420
00:43:59,220 --> 00:44:02,220
It'd take us a month to crack 
into this thing without a key.

421
00:44:02,310 --> 00:44:03,510
A key?

422
00:44:04,220 --> 00:44:07,270
A key! A key! That's what 
he was talking about!

423
00:44:07,350 --> 00:44:10,020
- Who? 
- The man on the barge,

424
00:44:10,100 --> 00:44:12,020
the one with the hook. He 
was looking for a key.

425
00:44:12,110 --> 00:44:13,780
Hey, that's mine.

426
00:44:18,860 --> 00:44:22,740
Seems that our American friends had a 
little misfortune of their own today.

427
00:44:23,080 --> 00:44:26,620
Three of their 
diggers were melted.

428
00:44:26,830 --> 00:44:28,580
- What? 
- How?

429
00:44:28,790 --> 00:44:30,170
Salt acid.

430
00:44:30,710 --> 00:44:35,250
Pressurized salt acid. Some 
kind of ancient booby trap.

431
00:44:36,170 --> 00:44:38,630
Maybe this place 
really is cursed.

432
00:44:41,970 --> 00:44:44,350
Oh, for goodness' sake, you two!

433
00:44:44,510 --> 00:44:46,720
- You don't believe in curses? 
- No, I don't.

434
00:44:46,810 --> 00:44:50,940
I believe if I can see it and I can touch 
it, it's real. That's what I believe.

435
00:44:51,230 --> 00:44:53,310
I believe in being prepared.

436
00:44:56,150 --> 00:44:58,700
Take this. Stay here.

437
00:44:58,860 --> 00:45:01,660
- No, wait, wait! Wait 
for me. Wait! - Evy!

438
00:45:01,740 --> 00:45:04,910
Excuse me, but didn't the 
man just say stay here?

439
00:45:04,990 --> 00:45:06,040
Evy!

440
00:45:09,120 --> 00:45:12,040
Mr. Henderson! Wake up!

441
00:45:51,710 --> 00:45:53,040
O'Connell!

442
00:46:12,560 --> 00:46:13,560
Enough!

443
00:46:15,270 --> 00:46:18,400
We will shed no more 
blood, but you must leave?

444
00:46:19,230 --> 00:46:21,240
Leave this place or die.

445
00:46:22,280 --> 00:46:23,950
You have one day.

446
00:46:47,720 --> 00:46:48,930
Evelyn.

447
00:46:51,270 --> 00:46:52,350
Hey.

448
00:46:54,230 --> 00:46:57,150
- Are you all right? 
- Yes, I'm fine.

449
00:46:57,940 --> 00:46:59,440
- You sure? 
- Thank you.

450
00:46:59,520 --> 00:47:03,440
That proves it. Old SETI's fortune's 
got to be under this sand.

451
00:47:03,610 --> 00:47:06,610
For them to protect it like this, you 
know there's treasure down there.

452
00:47:06,700 --> 00:47:10,620
No, these men are desert people. 
They value water, not gold.

453
00:47:11,160 --> 00:47:15,250
You know, maybe just at night, 
we could combine forces.

454
00:47:17,710 --> 00:47:19,710
Hey, tough stuff, 
try a right hook.

455
00:47:19,790 --> 00:47:22,340
Ball up your fist and put it... 
Put it up like that.

456
00:47:22,630 --> 00:47:24,380
And then mean it. 
Hit it right here.

457
00:47:24,470 --> 00:47:26,680
- Mean it! 
- Okay.

458
00:47:29,510 --> 00:47:31,970
Okay, it's time for 
another drink.

459
00:47:32,350 --> 00:47:34,640
Unlike my brother, Sir,

460
00:47:35,480 --> 00:47:37,810
I know when to say no.

461
00:47:40,150 --> 00:47:42,400
And unlike your brother, miss,

462
00:47:42,480 --> 00:47:44,360
you, I just don't get.

463
00:47:46,320 --> 00:47:49,200
I know. You're wondering

464
00:47:50,410 --> 00:47:54,500
what is a place like me 
doing in a girl like this.

465
00:47:54,870 --> 00:47:58,790
- Yeah, something like that. 
- Egypt is in my blood.

466
00:47:59,330 --> 00:48:00,590
You see,

467
00:48:00,670 --> 00:48:05,260
my father was a very, 
very famous explorer.

468
00:48:05,590 --> 00:48:07,720
And he loved Egypt so much,

469
00:48:08,010 --> 00:48:11,350
he married my mother, 
who was an Egyptian

470
00:48:11,680 --> 00:48:14,350
and quite an adventurer herself?

471
00:48:15,850 --> 00:48:19,520
I get your father and I 
get your mother, and...

472
00:48:21,230 --> 00:48:23,190
I get him, but...

473
00:48:24,230 --> 00:48:26,240
What are you doing here?

474
00:48:27,610 --> 00:48:31,870
Look, I may not be an explorer

475
00:48:32,280 --> 00:48:36,580
or an adventurer or 
a treasure seeker

476
00:48:36,660 --> 00:48:39,870
or a gunfighter, Mr. O'Connell,

477
00:48:40,580 --> 00:48:45,250
but I am proud of what I am.

478
00:48:46,050 --> 00:48:47,720
And what is that?

479
00:48:47,800 --> 00:48:48,880
I...

480
00:48:51,090 --> 00:48:53,050
am a librarian.

481
00:48:57,220 --> 00:49:00,230
And I am going to kiss you,

482
00:49:00,520 --> 00:49:02,900
- Mr. O'Connell. 
- Call me Rick.

483
00:49:09,450 --> 00:49:10,740
Rick.

484
00:49:47,110 --> 00:49:49,860
- There is a curse upon this 
chest. - Curse, my ass.

485
00:49:49,940 --> 00:49:52,700
- Who cares? - Have a 
care, Mr. Henderson.

486
00:49:52,780 --> 00:49:55,870
In these hallowed grounds, that which 
was set forth in ancient times

487
00:49:55,950 --> 00:49:58,410
- is as strong today as it 
was then. - We understand.

488
00:49:58,490 --> 00:50:00,450
What's it say?

489
00:50:07,630 --> 00:50:10,210
"Death will come on swift wings"

490
00:50:10,300 --> 00:50:13,090
"to whomsoever 
opens this chest."

491
00:50:24,480 --> 00:50:26,400
We should not be here.

492
00:50:27,190 --> 00:50:28,770
This is not good.

493
00:50:28,860 --> 00:50:30,230
It says,

494
00:50:30,320 --> 00:50:32,240
"There is one, the undead,"

495
00:50:32,950 --> 00:50:36,320
"who, if brought back to life, 
is bound by sacred law"

496
00:50:36,410 --> 00:50:38,370
"to consummate this curse."

497
00:50:38,450 --> 00:50:41,290
Let's make sure we don't bring 
anyone back from the dead then.

498
00:50:41,370 --> 00:50:44,330
"He will kill all who 
open this chest"

499
00:50:44,830 --> 00:50:47,590
"and assimilate their 
organs and fluids,"

500
00:50:49,050 --> 00:50:51,590
"and in so doing, 
he will regenerate"

501
00:50:51,920 --> 00:50:54,130
"and no longer be the undead,"

502
00:50:54,680 --> 00:50:57,180
"but a plague upon this Earth."

503
00:50:59,100 --> 00:51:02,270
Well, we didn't come all 
this way for nothing.

504
00:51:02,350 --> 00:51:04,600
- That's right. 
- It's the curse.

505
00:51:04,890 --> 00:51:06,440
It's the curse.

506
00:51:06,520 --> 00:51:10,320
It's the curse! 
Beware of the curse!

507
00:51:10,400 --> 00:51:11,940
Beware!

508
00:51:12,030 --> 00:51:14,780
Stupid superstitious bastard.

509
00:51:17,070 --> 00:51:19,780
Oh, I've dreamt about this 
since I was a little girl.

510
00:51:19,870 --> 00:51:21,290
You dream about dead guys?

511
00:51:21,370 --> 00:51:24,460
Look, the sacred spells 
have been chiseled off.

512
00:51:24,830 --> 00:51:27,960
This man must have been condemned not 
only in this life, but in the next.

513
00:51:28,040 --> 00:51:30,210
- Tough break. - Yeah, 
I'm all tears.

514
00:51:30,290 --> 00:51:33,710
Now, let's see who's 
inside, shall we?

515
00:51:44,310 --> 00:51:46,980
Oh, my God, I hate it when 
these things do that.

516
00:51:47,060 --> 00:51:48,520
Is he supposed to 
look like that?

517
00:51:48,600 --> 00:51:51,020
No, I've never seen a mummy 
look like this before.

518
00:51:51,110 --> 00:51:53,860
He's still... Still...

519
00:51:53,940 --> 00:51:54,990
- Juicy. 
- Juicy.

520
00:51:55,070 --> 00:51:56,320
Yes.

521
00:52:04,700 --> 00:52:06,080
Oh, my God.

522
00:52:06,870 --> 00:52:08,870
It does exist.

523
00:52:08,960 --> 00:52:11,790
- The Book of the Dead. 
- A book?

524
00:52:12,380 --> 00:52:15,010
Who cares about a book? Where 
the hell's the treasure?

525
00:52:15,090 --> 00:52:16,720
This, gentlemen.

526
00:52:17,930 --> 00:52:20,090
This is treasure.

527
00:52:21,550 --> 00:52:24,390
Hell, I wouldn't trade 
you for a brass...

528
00:52:27,100 --> 00:52:29,690
Look at that.

529
00:52:30,060 --> 00:52:32,310
There's your 
treasure, gentlemen.

530
00:52:32,400 --> 00:52:34,400
Now, we're on to something.

531
00:52:50,370 --> 00:52:53,250
I believe you need a 
key to open that book.

532
00:52:55,380 --> 00:52:56,510
Say, O'Connell,

533
00:52:56,590 --> 00:52:59,010
what do you think these 
babies'll fetch back home?

534
00:52:59,090 --> 00:53:03,220
We hear you boys found yourselves a 
nice, gooey mummy. Congratulations.

535
00:53:03,300 --> 00:53:07,640
If you dry that fellow out, you might 
be able to sell him for firewood.

536
00:53:38,460 --> 00:53:40,670
That's called 
stealing, you know?

537
00:53:40,760 --> 00:53:44,510
According to you and my 
brother, it's called borrowing.

538
00:53:47,100 --> 00:53:50,020
I thought the Book of Amun-Ra 
was made out of gold?

539
00:53:50,140 --> 00:53:54,060
It is made out of gold. This 
isn't the Book of Amun-Ra.

540
00:53:54,150 --> 00:53:56,320
This is something else.

541
00:53:56,440 --> 00:53:59,320
I think this may be 
the Book of the Dead.

542
00:53:59,650 --> 00:54:01,360
The Book of the Dead?

543
00:54:01,450 --> 00:54:03,780
Are you sure you're meant to be 
playing around with this thing?

544
00:54:03,860 --> 00:54:05,700
It's just a book.

545
00:54:05,870 --> 00:54:09,620
No harm ever came 
from reading a book.

546
00:54:15,290 --> 00:54:17,710
That happens a lot around here.

547
00:54:20,170 --> 00:54:21,550
So, what's it say?

548
00:54:21,630 --> 00:54:24,760
"Amun Ra. Amun Dei."

549
00:54:25,590 --> 00:54:28,310
It speaks of the night 
and of the day.

550
00:54:42,400 --> 00:54:46,240
No! You must not 
read from the book!

551
00:55:12,850 --> 00:55:14,230
Run!

552
00:55:16,690 --> 00:55:18,360
Go, go!

553
00:55:26,820 --> 00:55:29,030
What have we done?

554
00:55:30,120 --> 00:55:34,040
- Where the hell did they come from? 
- I ain't waiting around to find out.

555
00:55:41,000 --> 00:55:42,380
Oh, Jon!

556
00:55:48,720 --> 00:55:50,140
Scarabs!

557
00:55:50,350 --> 00:55:53,390
Run, Evy! Go, go, go! Run!

558
00:56:09,620 --> 00:56:11,910
I told you to leave or die.

559
00:56:12,280 --> 00:56:13,830
You refused.

560
00:56:14,040 --> 00:56:15,950
Now, you may have killed us all,

561
00:56:16,040 --> 00:56:19,330
for you have unleashed a creasture we 
have feared for more than 3,000 years.

562
00:56:22,960 --> 00:56:26,420
I must now go on the hunt, and 
try and find a way to kill him.

563
00:56:59,210 --> 00:57:02,750
May the good Lord protect 
and watch over me

564
00:57:02,840 --> 00:57:05,340
as a Shepherd watches 
over his flock.

565
00:57:19,180 --> 00:57:21,190
No. Okay.

566
00:57:40,790 --> 00:57:42,120
The language of the slaves...

567
00:57:44,130 --> 00:57:47,130
I may have use for you.

568
00:57:48,760 --> 00:57:50,840
And the rewards...

569
00:57:51,630 --> 00:57:54,300
will be great.

570
00:57:56,010 --> 00:57:57,680
My Prince.

571
00:58:00,480 --> 00:58:04,650
Where are the other 
sacred jars...

572
00:58:26,080 --> 00:58:29,000
I thought you said you didn't believe 
in that fairy tales and hokum stuff.

573
00:58:29,460 --> 00:58:33,840
Having an encounter with a 
3,000-year-old walking, talking corpse

574
00:58:33,930 --> 00:58:35,260
does tend to convert one.

575
00:58:35,340 --> 00:58:38,680
Forget it. We're out the door, 
down the hall, and we're gone.

576
00:58:38,760 --> 00:58:41,850
- Oh, no, we are not. 
- Oh, yes, we are.

577
00:58:41,930 --> 00:58:44,690
Oh, no, we are not. We woke him 
up, and we are going to stop him.

578
00:58:44,770 --> 00:58:46,860
We? What we? We didn't 
read that book.

579
00:58:46,940 --> 00:58:49,610
I told you not to play around with 
that thing. Didn't I tell you?

580
00:58:49,690 --> 00:58:52,860
Yes, right then, me, me, me, me. I, I, I 
woke him up and I intend to stop him.

581
00:58:52,950 --> 00:58:54,360
Yeah? How?

582
00:58:54,450 --> 00:58:57,070
You heard the man. No mortal 
weapons can kill this guy.

583
00:58:57,160 --> 00:59:00,370
Then we are just going to have 
to find some immortal ones.

584
00:59:00,450 --> 00:59:01,910
There goes that we again.

585
00:59:01,100 --> 00:59:04,370
Look, will you listen to me? 
We have to do something.

586
00:59:04,460 --> 00:59:07,080
Once this creature has been reborn, 
his curse is going to spread

587
00:59:07,170 --> 00:59:08,710
until the whole of the 
Earth is destroyed.

588
00:59:08,790 --> 00:59:11,800
- Yeah? Is that my problem? 
- Lt is everybody's problem.

589
00:59:11,880 --> 00:59:14,170
Evelyn, I appreciate you 
saving my life and all,

590
00:59:14,260 --> 00:59:17,550
but when I signed on, I agreed to take 
you out there and to bring you back.

591
00:59:17,640 --> 00:59:20,970
I have done that? End of job. End 
of story. Contract terminated.

592
00:59:21,060 --> 00:59:22,270
That's all I am to you? 
A contract?

593
00:59:22,350 --> 00:59:27,230
You can either tag along with me or you 
can stay here and try and save the world.

594
00:59:27,480 --> 00:59:29,940
- What's it gonna be? 
- I'm staying.

595
00:59:30,020 --> 00:59:33,150
- Fine. 
- Fine.

596
00:59:33,320 --> 00:59:34,530
Fine.

597
00:59:40,700 --> 00:59:44,790
I'm the last of the Royal Air Corps 
still stationed out here, you know...

598
00:59:45,160 --> 00:59:47,870
Some bloody idiot 
spilt his drink.

599
00:59:48,170 --> 00:59:52,130
All the other laddies died in the 
sky and were buried in the sand.

600
00:59:52,210 --> 00:59:54,720
Good chaps, every 
one of them, too.

601
00:59:55,010 --> 00:59:57,630
- Hi, Winston. 
- Yeah...

602
00:59:57,720 --> 01:00:01,390
You know, O'Connell, ever since 
the end of the great war,

603
01:00:01,470 --> 01:00:06,390
there hasn't been a single 
challenge worthy of a man like me.

604
01:00:06,690 --> 01:00:09,150
Yeah? We all got our little 
problems today, Winston.

605
01:00:09,230 --> 01:00:12,690
I just wish I could have 
chucked it in with the others

606
01:00:12,770 --> 01:00:14,940
and gone down in 
flame and Glory,

607
01:00:15,030 --> 01:00:17,450
instead of sitting 
around here, rotting

608
01:00:17,530 --> 01:00:19,110
- of boredom and booze. 
- Of boredom and booze.

609
01:00:19,200 --> 01:00:20,700
Cheers.

610
01:00:23,290 --> 01:00:25,660
Oh, well, back to the airfield.

611
01:00:27,540 --> 01:00:30,130
- Tell me, has your sister always 
been... - Oh, yes, always.

612
01:00:30,210 --> 01:00:33,210
We're all packed up, but the damn boat 
doesn't leave till tomorrow morning.

613
01:00:33,300 --> 01:00:35,630
Tail set firmly between 
your legs, I see.

614
01:00:35,710 --> 01:00:39,180
You can talk. You don't have some 
sacred walking corpse after you.

615
01:00:41,300 --> 01:00:43,060
Sweet Jesus!

616
01:00:43,600 --> 01:00:45,520
Tasted just like...

617
01:00:46,520 --> 01:00:47,890
Blood.

618
01:00:47,980 --> 01:00:53,400
"And the rivers and waters of 
Egypt ran red and were as blood."

619
01:00:55,570 --> 01:00:57,190
He's here.

620
01:01:01,700 --> 01:01:03,620
Oh, looks like it's gonna storm.

621
01:01:03,700 --> 01:01:06,750
- Oh, Evelyn! - Oh, 
so you're still here.

622
01:01:07,580 --> 01:01:09,370
We've got problems.

623
01:01:31,900 --> 01:01:33,150
Hey!

624
01:01:33,900 --> 01:01:36,820
Beni, you little stinkweed. 
Where you been?

625
01:02:02,010 --> 01:02:04,090
We are in serious trouble.

626
01:02:22,280 --> 01:02:23,780
You saved me from the undead.

627
01:02:23,860 --> 01:02:25,030
I thank you.

628
01:02:41,470 --> 01:02:44,800
We are in very serious trouble.

629
01:02:46,640 --> 01:02:48,930
- He does seem to like you Evy. 
- Yeah, what's that about?

630
01:02:48,970 --> 01:02:50,180
What's this guy want?

631
01:02:50,270 --> 01:02:53,350
There's only one person that 
can give us any answers.

632
01:02:53,480 --> 01:02:55,980
- You! 
- Miss Carnahan.

633
01:02:56,520 --> 01:02:59,270
- Gentlemen. - What 
is he doing here?

634
01:02:59,570 --> 01:03:03,240
Do you really want to know, or 
would you prefer to just shoot us?

635
01:03:03,320 --> 01:03:07,320
After what I just saw, I'm willing 
to go on a little faith here.

636
01:03:07,410 --> 01:03:09,990
We are part of an 
ancient secret society.

637
01:03:10,080 --> 01:03:13,790
For over 3,000 yeasrs, we have 
guarded the City of the Dead.

638
01:03:13,870 --> 01:03:18,170
We are sworn at manhood to do any and all 
in our part to stop the High Priest Imhotep

639
01:03:18,250 --> 01:03:19,750
from being reborn 
into this world.

640
01:03:19,840 --> 01:03:21,420
Because of you, we have failed.

641
01:03:21,510 --> 01:03:24,090
You think this justifies the 
killing of innocent people.

642
01:03:24,170 --> 01:03:26,090
To stop this creature? 
Let me think.

643
01:03:26,180 --> 01:03:27,510
- Yes! 
- Yes!

644
01:03:29,140 --> 01:03:31,890
Question. Why doesn't 
he like cats?

645
01:03:31,970 --> 01:03:33,890
Cats are the guardians 
of the underworld.

646
01:03:33,980 --> 01:03:35,850
He will fear them until 
he is fully regenerated?

647
01:03:35,890 --> 01:03:37,150
Then he will fear nothing.

648
01:03:37,230 --> 01:03:39,110
You know how he gets his 
self fully regenerated?

649
01:03:39,190 --> 01:03:43,240
- By killing everyone who opened 
that chest. - And sucking them dry!

650
01:03:43,360 --> 01:03:45,740
Jonathan, will you stop 
playing with that.

651
01:03:45,820 --> 01:03:50,620
When I saw him alive at Hamunaptra, 
he called me Anck-Su-Namun.

652
01:03:52,540 --> 01:03:55,660
And then juat now in Mr. Burns' 
quarters, he tried to kiss me.

653
01:03:55,750 --> 01:03:58,670
It's because of his love for 
Anck-Su-Namun that he was cursed.

654
01:03:58,750 --> 01:04:01,670
- Apparently, even after 3,000 years... 
- He is still in love with her.

655
01:04:01,750 --> 01:04:04,300
Yes, that is very romantic, but 
what has it got to do with me?

656
01:04:04,380 --> 01:04:06,680
Perhaps he will once again try 
to raise her from the dead.

657
01:04:06,720 --> 01:04:07,760
Yes.

658
01:04:07,840 --> 01:04:11,010
And it appears he has already 
chosen his human sacrifice.

659
01:04:13,890 --> 01:04:15,730
Bad luck, old mum.

660
01:04:15,810 --> 01:04:17,480
On the contrary,

661
01:04:17,560 --> 01:04:21,230
it may just give us the time 
we need to kill the creature.

662
01:04:21,310 --> 01:04:23,940
We will need all the 
help we can get.

663
01:04:24,690 --> 01:04:26,610
His powers are growing.

664
01:04:31,410 --> 01:04:35,080
"And he stretched forth his 
hand towards the heavens"

665
01:04:35,160 --> 01:04:39,000
"and there was darkness 
throughout the land of Egypt."

666
01:04:39,870 --> 01:04:41,500
We must stop him 
from regenerating.

667
01:04:41,590 --> 01:04:42,670
Who opened that chest?

668
01:04:42,750 --> 01:04:44,300
There was me and Daniels here.

669
01:04:44,380 --> 01:04:46,720
- And Burns, of course. - And 
that Egyptologist fellow.

670
01:04:46,800 --> 01:04:47,970
What about my buddy, Beni?

671
01:04:48,050 --> 01:04:50,300
No, he scrammed out of there 
before we opened the thing.

672
01:04:50,390 --> 01:04:53,350
- Yeah, he was the smart one. 
- Yeah, that sounds like Beni.

673
01:04:53,430 --> 01:04:56,810
We must find the Egyptologist and bring 
him back to the safety of the fort

674
01:04:56,890 --> 01:04:59,060
- before the creature can 
get to him. - Right.

675
01:04:59,140 --> 01:05:00,730
She stays here. You 
three, come with me.

676
01:05:00,810 --> 01:05:02,690
- Not me... 
- No, no!

677
01:05:02,770 --> 01:05:06,690
Just a minute! You can't leave me 
behind like some old carpetbag.

678
01:05:06,780 --> 01:05:10,110
Who put you in charge, O'Connell? 
What do you think you are doing?

679
01:05:10,200 --> 01:05:11,740
Jonathan! O'Connell!

680
01:05:11,820 --> 01:05:14,620
- Sorry, but he's a bit tall. 
- Jonathan, coward that you are...

681
01:05:14,700 --> 01:05:17,160
O'Connell, you are not 
leaving me in here!

682
01:05:17,700 --> 01:05:20,580
Jonathan, if you don't open 
this door in one minute flat...

683
01:05:20,670 --> 01:05:22,630
- O'Connell... - This 
door doesn't open.

684
01:05:22,710 --> 01:05:24,210
- Let me out of here. 
- She doesn't come out,

685
01:05:24,290 --> 01:05:26,250
- and no one goes in, right? 
- Right.

686
01:05:26,340 --> 01:05:28,010
- Right? 
- Right.

687
01:05:28,090 --> 01:05:30,260
O'Connell! Jonathan!

688
01:05:30,340 --> 01:05:31,590
Let's go, Jonathan?

689
01:05:31,680 --> 01:05:34,800
Oh, I thought I could just astay 
at the fort and reconnoiter.

690
01:05:34,890 --> 01:05:36,180
- Now! 
- Yeah, right.

691
01:05:36,260 --> 01:05:38,730
We're just gonna rescue 
the Egyptologist.

692
01:06:11,510 --> 01:06:15,180
Well, well, well. Let me guess. 
Spring cleaning?

693
01:06:19,350 --> 01:06:20,730
Nice shot.

694
01:06:21,520 --> 01:06:24,690
Oh, Beni, did you fall down? 
Let me help you up.

695
01:06:27,070 --> 01:06:30,650
You came back from the desert with 
a new friend, didn't you, Beni?

696
01:06:30,740 --> 01:06:33,240
What friend? You are 
my only friend.

697
01:06:35,620 --> 01:06:39,450
What the hell are you doing with 
this creep? What's in it for you?

698
01:06:39,540 --> 01:06:43,540
It is better to be the right hand 
of the devil than in his path.

699
01:06:43,620 --> 01:06:46,880
As long as I serve 
him, I am immune.

700
01:06:50,550 --> 01:06:51,970
Immune from what?

701
01:06:53,470 --> 01:06:54,550
What did you say?

702
01:06:54,630 --> 01:06:57,470
I don't wanna tell you. You'll 
just hurt me some more.

703
01:06:57,550 --> 01:07:00,430
What are you looking for? 
And try not to lie to me.

704
01:07:00,810 --> 01:07:02,140
The book!

705
01:07:02,230 --> 01:07:05,400
The black book they found at 
Hamunaptra. He wants it back.

706
01:07:05,480 --> 01:07:07,650
He said to me it would be 
worth it's weight in gold.

707
01:07:07,730 --> 01:07:10,400
- What does he want the 
book for? - I don't know.

708
01:07:10,900 --> 01:07:14,320
Something about bringing his 
dead girlfriend back to life.

709
01:07:14,400 --> 01:07:16,820
But that's all. He just 
wants the book, I swear.

710
01:07:16,910 --> 01:07:20,330
Just the book, I swear. 
And your sister.

711
01:07:20,450 --> 01:07:21,620
But other than that...

712
01:08:12,630 --> 01:08:16,510
- That's two down, two to go. 
- Then he'll be coming after Evy.

713
01:08:16,670 --> 01:08:18,220
Guards in place!

714
01:08:18,300 --> 01:08:19,840
Reporting all clear, Sir!

715
01:08:19,930 --> 01:08:21,430
To hell with this?

716
01:08:21,510 --> 01:08:23,930
I'm going downstairs, get me 
a drink. You want something?

717
01:08:24,020 --> 01:08:27,230
- Yeah. Yeah, get me a glass 
of bourbon. - All right.

718
01:08:27,310 --> 01:08:29,600
- And a shot of bourbon. 
- Yeah, okay, okay.

719
01:08:29,690 --> 01:08:32,230
- And as bourbon chasser. 
- I'll get your damn bourbon!

720
01:08:32,320 --> 01:08:34,480
Don't worry about the door.

721
01:10:13,920 --> 01:10:15,920
Anck-Su-Namun.

722
01:10:26,850 --> 01:10:29,600
Hey, get your ugly 
face off of her!

723
01:10:33,140 --> 01:10:34,940
Look what I got.

724
01:10:51,790 --> 01:10:55,210
- You all right? 
- Well, I'm not sure.

725
01:11:05,510 --> 01:11:09,510
Well, according to legend, the black 
book the Americans found at Hamunaptra

726
01:11:09,600 --> 01:11:11,640
is supposed to bring 
people back from the dead.

727
01:11:11,720 --> 01:11:13,850
Until now, it was a notion I 
was unwilling to believe.

728
01:11:13,850 --> 01:11:16,400
Believe it, sister. That's what 
brought our buddy back to life?

729
01:11:16,480 --> 01:11:19,860
Yes. I'm thinking that if the black 
book can bring dead people to life...

730
01:11:19,940 --> 01:11:23,610
- Then maybe the gold book can 
kill him. - That's the myth.

731
01:11:23,700 --> 01:11:26,570
Now, we just have to find out 
where the gold book is hidden.

732
01:11:26,660 --> 01:11:28,280
Imhotep.

733
01:11:28,660 --> 01:11:30,240
Imhotep.

734
01:11:30,450 --> 01:11:32,160
Imhotep.

735
01:11:32,500 --> 01:11:34,250
Imhotep.

736
01:11:34,750 --> 01:11:36,540
Imhotep.

737
01:11:36,830 --> 01:11:40,920
Imhotep.

738
01:11:41,170 --> 01:11:47,090
Imhotep.

739
01:11:47,550 --> 01:11:49,350
Imhotep.

740
01:11:49,800 --> 01:11:51,010
Imhotep.

741
01:11:51,100 --> 01:11:55,100
Last but not least, my favorite 
plague, boils and sores.

742
01:11:55,180 --> 01:11:57,190
They have become his slaves.

743
01:11:57,560 --> 01:12:00,860
So it has begun, the 
beginning of the end.

744
01:12:00,940 --> 01:12:03,530
Not quite yet, it hasn't. 
Come on.

745
01:12:04,650 --> 01:12:06,360
Imhotep.

746
01:12:06,860 --> 01:12:08,700
Imhotep.

747
01:12:08,820 --> 01:12:10,370
Imhotep.

748
01:12:13,370 --> 01:12:16,910
Imhotep.

749
01:12:17,750 --> 01:12:19,380
Imhotep.

750
01:12:26,380 --> 01:12:29,220
According to Bembridge scholars, 
the Golden Book of Amun-Ra

751
01:12:29,300 --> 01:12:31,300
is located inside the 
statue of Anubis.

752
01:12:31,390 --> 01:12:33,390
- That's where we found the 
black book. - Exactly.

753
01:12:33,470 --> 01:12:35,350
Looks like the old boys at 
Bembridge were mistaken.

754
01:12:35,430 --> 01:12:37,060
They mixed the books up.

755
01:12:37,140 --> 01:12:38,940
Mixed up where they were buried.

756
01:12:39,020 --> 01:12:42,070
So, if the black book is 
inside the statue of Anubis,

757
01:12:42,150 --> 01:12:44,820
then the Golden book 
must be inside...

758
01:12:48,570 --> 01:12:51,990
- I think I'll go and get the 
car started. - I've got it.

759
01:12:52,080 --> 01:12:56,200
The Golden Book of Amun-Ra is at 
Hamunaptra inside the statue of Horus.

760
01:13:05,420 --> 01:13:07,590
Imhotep.

761
01:13:08,220 --> 01:13:10,800
Imhotep.

762
01:13:10,970 --> 01:13:15,680
Imhotep.

763
01:13:15,970 --> 01:13:18,100
Imhotep.

764
01:13:24,980 --> 01:13:27,440
Let's go, let's go, let's go!

765
01:13:29,200 --> 01:13:31,740
Get this thing in gear, boy. 
Let's get out of here.

766
01:13:31,820 --> 01:13:34,160
- Evy. Come on, Evy. Hurry up. 
- Imhotep!

767
01:13:34,330 --> 01:13:35,830
Imhotep!

768
01:13:42,460 --> 01:13:45,340
You're gonna get yours, Beni! 
You hear me?

769
01:13:45,920 --> 01:13:50,340
- You're gonna get yours! - Oh, 
like I've never heard that before!

770
01:14:20,710 --> 01:14:22,620
Hang on!

771
01:14:58,240 --> 01:15:00,950
Hey! O'Connell! O'Connell!

772
01:15:56,470 --> 01:15:57,640
Jump down!

773
01:16:00,640 --> 01:16:02,470
Move, move, move!

774
01:16:05,310 --> 01:16:07,020
Imhotep.

775
01:16:07,100 --> 01:16:08,690
Imhotep.

776
01:16:09,110 --> 01:16:10,650
Imhotep.

777
01:16:11,150 --> 01:16:16,320
Imhotep.

778
01:16:16,820 --> 01:16:18,610
Imhotep.

779
01:16:21,580 --> 01:16:23,330
It's the creature.

780
01:16:23,910 --> 01:16:25,750
He's fully regenerated.

781
01:16:31,130 --> 01:16:33,170
Come with me, my Princess.

782
01:16:33,340 --> 01:16:36,510
It is time to make 
you mine forever.

783
01:16:36,720 --> 01:16:39,010
For all eternity, idiot.

784
01:16:45,140 --> 01:16:48,480
Take my hand, and I will 
spare your friends.

785
01:16:49,690 --> 01:16:51,110
Oh, dear.

786
01:16:53,400 --> 01:16:56,110
- Have you got any bright ideas? 
- I'm thinking, I'm thinking.

787
01:16:56,190 --> 01:16:59,610
You better think of something fast, 
because if he turns me into a mummy,

788
01:16:59,700 --> 01:17:02,030
you're the first one 
I'm coming after.

789
01:17:06,040 --> 01:17:07,120
No.

790
01:17:07,200 --> 01:17:08,250
- Don't. 
- No!

791
01:17:08,330 --> 01:17:10,620
He has to take me to Hamunaptra 
to perform the ritual.

792
01:17:10,710 --> 01:17:14,340
She is right. Live 
today, fight tomorrow.

793
01:17:25,850 --> 01:17:28,560
I'll be seeing you again.

794
01:17:34,690 --> 01:17:36,030
Evelyn!

795
01:17:38,740 --> 01:17:41,910
- Hey, that's mine. 
- Thank you.

796
01:17:43,320 --> 01:17:44,580
Kill them all!

797
01:17:44,660 --> 01:17:46,870
No! Let go of me!

798
01:17:46,950 --> 01:17:48,540
- Imhotep. 
- Let go of me!

799
01:17:48,620 --> 01:17:51,580
- Goodbye, my friend. 
- Come here, you little...

800
01:17:51,670 --> 01:17:54,540
Imhotep.

801
01:17:54,920 --> 01:17:56,750
Imhotep.

802
01:18:00,550 --> 01:18:01,680
Come on!

803
01:18:01,760 --> 01:18:03,760
- What about my sister? 
- We're gonna get her back!

804
01:18:03,760 --> 01:18:05,470
Go!

805
01:18:05,550 --> 01:18:06,930
You're next!

806
01:18:08,430 --> 01:18:10,350
Come on! Give me your hand!

807
01:18:10,430 --> 01:18:12,600
- You go! 
- Come on!

808
01:18:12,690 --> 01:18:13,940
Go!

809
01:18:46,640 --> 01:18:49,640
Morning, Winston. A word?

810
01:18:51,140 --> 01:18:54,900
What's your little problem got to do 
with His Majesty's Royal Air Corps?

811
01:18:54,980 --> 01:18:56,520
Not a damn thing.

812
01:18:58,360 --> 01:19:00,900
- Is it dasngerous? - You 
probably won't live through it.

813
01:19:00,990 --> 01:19:02,900
By Jove, do you really think so?

814
01:19:02,990 --> 01:19:05,490
Everybody else we've bumped 
into has died. Why not you?

815
01:19:05,570 --> 01:19:08,240
What's the... What's 
the challenge, then?

816
01:19:08,330 --> 01:19:11,100
Rescue the damsel in distress, kill 
the bad guy, and save the world.

817
01:19:13,460 --> 01:19:15,920
Winston Havlock at 
your service, Sir.

818
01:19:27,180 --> 01:19:29,180
Are you all right?

819
01:19:30,060 --> 01:19:32,520
Do I bloody look all right?

820
01:19:36,560 --> 01:19:38,150
How you doing?

821
01:19:47,780 --> 01:19:51,410
See that? I've never 
seen one so big.

822
01:19:52,040 --> 01:19:53,950
- Never? 
- No!

823
01:20:06,760 --> 01:20:09,890
Get off me! Get off!

824
01:20:11,640 --> 01:20:13,520
I need a new job.

825
01:20:34,410 --> 01:20:35,960
Oh, my God.

826
01:20:36,410 --> 01:20:37,830
We're back.

827
01:20:50,340 --> 01:20:51,930
O'Connell.

828
01:21:04,480 --> 01:21:06,780
Oh, my God.

829
01:21:08,400 --> 01:21:12,370
Hey, Winston! Pedal faster!

830
01:21:14,580 --> 01:21:16,330
Hang on, men!

831
01:21:59,830 --> 01:22:02,040
Stop it! You'll kill them!

832
01:22:02,170 --> 01:22:03,830
That's the idea.

833
01:22:19,060 --> 01:22:21,440
Here I come, laddies!

834
01:22:48,840 --> 01:22:52,220
I love the whole sand-wall 
trick. It was beautiful.

835
01:22:52,300 --> 01:22:53,550
Bastard.

836
01:23:11,610 --> 01:23:13,570
Yeah, yeah. All right.

837
01:23:19,120 --> 01:23:21,700
Winston! Hey, Winston!

838
01:23:36,220 --> 01:23:39,760
Quicksand! Get back! 
It's quicksand?

839
01:24:53,380 --> 01:24:56,260
Bembridge scholars never 
wrote about this.

840
01:25:01,260 --> 01:25:03,680
Get off me. Get off!

841
01:25:49,600 --> 01:25:51,900
Wha... Wha...

842
01:26:12,830 --> 01:26:14,590
- Can you see... 
- Yeah.

843
01:26:14,670 --> 01:26:17,000
- Can you believe... 
- Yeah.

844
01:26:17,090 --> 01:26:18,840
- Can we just... 
- No.

845
01:26:29,680 --> 01:26:32,900
- Who the hell are these guys? 
- Priests.

846
01:26:33,350 --> 01:26:34,860
Imhotep's priests.

847
01:26:35,270 --> 01:26:36,820
All right, then.

848
01:27:24,740 --> 01:27:26,240
There he is!

849
01:27:27,410 --> 01:27:29,370
Hello, Horus, old boy.

850
01:27:38,590 --> 01:27:40,550
Time to close the door.

851
01:28:20,790 --> 01:28:22,500
Anck-Su-Namun.

852
01:28:31,640 --> 01:28:33,100
O'Connell!

853
01:28:33,850 --> 01:28:35,310
Jonathan!

854
01:28:46,150 --> 01:28:49,070
Damn. These guys just 
don't quit, do they?

855
01:28:49,200 --> 01:28:50,740
Keep digging.

856
01:29:14,560 --> 01:29:16,180
The Book of Amun-Ra.

857
01:29:24,610 --> 01:29:27,440
Save the girl. 
Kill the creature.

858
01:29:45,630 --> 01:29:47,550
You all right. Let's go.

859
01:30:42,600 --> 01:30:46,360
The Book of Amun-Ra! I 
found it, Evy! I found it!

860
01:30:48,150 --> 01:30:51,030
Shut up and get me 
off here, Jonathan!

861
01:30:51,360 --> 01:30:54,950
Open the book, Jonathan. It's 
the only way to kill him.

862
01:30:55,030 --> 01:30:57,830
You have to open the book 
and find the inscription.

863
01:30:57,910 --> 01:31:01,710
Well, I can't open it! 
It's locked or something.

864
01:31:03,460 --> 01:31:06,790
- We need the key, Evy! 
- It's inside his robes.

865
01:31:13,680 --> 01:31:14,930
O'Connell!

866
01:31:46,790 --> 01:31:48,170
Mummies.

867
01:31:59,640 --> 01:32:02,520
Look out! There's one there... 
O'Connell!

868
01:32:28,080 --> 01:32:30,170
Here's an inscription.

869
01:32:49,230 --> 01:32:50,860
Oh, boy.

870
01:32:57,820 --> 01:33:01,200
Oh, yeah. This just keeps 
getting better and better.

871
01:33:01,870 --> 01:33:04,040
- Do something, Jonathan. 
- Me?

872
01:33:04,120 --> 01:33:07,080
- You can command them. - You 
have got to be joking.

873
01:33:07,160 --> 01:33:10,670
Finish the inscription on the cover, 
idiot, then you can control them.

874
01:33:10,750 --> 01:33:12,590
Oh. Right.

875
01:33:50,250 --> 01:33:52,130
Hurry up, Jonathan!

876
01:34:23,990 --> 01:34:27,660
- I can't figure out this last 
symbol. - What does it look like?

877
01:34:41,760 --> 01:34:45,140
It's a bird. A stork!

878
01:34:58,020 --> 01:34:59,860
Ahmenophus.

879
01:35:01,570 --> 01:35:03,150
Oh, yes, I see.

880
01:35:49,030 --> 01:35:50,870
Anck-Su-Namun!

881
01:36:01,960 --> 01:36:03,550
Anck-Su-Namun!

882
01:36:31,120 --> 01:36:33,500
Evy, I've got it.

883
01:36:38,120 --> 01:36:39,630
Keep him busy.

884
01:36:43,460 --> 01:36:44,960
No problem.

885
01:36:57,350 --> 01:37:00,810
- Hurry, Evy! Hurry! 
- You're not helping.

886
01:37:09,860 --> 01:37:11,410
Oh! I've got it?

887
01:37:41,310 --> 01:37:44,480
I thought you said it 
was gonna kill him!

888
01:37:53,580 --> 01:37:55,030
He's mortal.

889
01:38:30,320 --> 01:38:33,410
Death is only the beginning.

890
01:39:03,560 --> 01:39:04,980
Time to go.

891
01:39:15,660 --> 01:39:18,490
You've lost the book! 
Jonathan, I can't believe...

892
01:39:18,580 --> 01:39:20,080
- Hurry! 
- Come on!

893
01:39:48,560 --> 01:39:51,360
- Couldn't we just... 
- No, Jonathan!

894
01:39:57,660 --> 01:39:59,160
O'Connell!

895
01:40:01,830 --> 01:40:05,040
- O'Connell! Wait! 
- Come on, come on!

896
01:40:05,290 --> 01:40:08,330
- Wait! Please! - Come 
on, come on! Beni!

897
01:40:10,170 --> 01:40:11,750
Goodbye, Beni.

898
01:40:14,880 --> 01:40:16,680
Look out, look out!

899
01:41:03,640 --> 01:41:06,100
Thank you. Thank you very much?

900
01:41:06,180 --> 01:41:10,020
You have earned the respect and 
gratitude of me and my people?

901
01:41:10,100 --> 01:41:12,820
Yes, well, it was nothing?

902
01:41:12,900 --> 01:41:15,190
May Allah smile upon you always.

903
01:41:17,240 --> 01:41:19,360
And yourself.

904
01:41:25,450 --> 01:41:28,250
- Yes, anytime. - Stay 
out of trouble.

905
01:41:29,330 --> 01:41:32,080
He's just leaving us here?

906
01:41:36,420 --> 01:41:40,220
Well, I guess we go home 
empty-handed again.

907
01:41:40,590 --> 01:41:42,390
I wouldn't say that.

908
01:41:44,600 --> 01:41:46,600
Oh, please!

909
01:41:55,730 --> 01:41:58,490
How about you, darling? Would 
you like a little kissy-wissy?
